  <RESPONSE>
    You're right, I misunderstood — you want the lock screen to activate (showing the slideshow) but dismiss without a password. Good news: that option exists.
    
    **System Settings → Screen Locking → "Allow unlocking without password for [X] minutes"**
    
    Set the time window to something large (or the maximum), and the lock screen will activate normally after idle but let you swipe/click past it without a password. It functions as a pure slideshow that way.
    
    **One caveat to know about:** There's a [known bug in kscreenlocker](https://discuss.kde.org/t/kscreenlocker-no-password-screen-unlock-will-generate-authentication-failure/19257) where password-free unlocks log an "authentication failure" to the system journal and can increment the PAM bad-login counter. Functionally it works, but if you have a PAM lockout policy it could theoretically block logins after enough dismissals. Most default desktop setups don't have that policy, so in practice it's usually fine — just worth knowing.
    
    So the full setup becomes:
    1. Build the Jellyfin QML wallpaper plugin → apply to lock screen
    2. Screen Locking → set idle timeout (e.g. 5 min)
    3. Screen Locking → "Allow unlocking without password for" → set to max/always
    4. Result: idle → lock screen activates with Jellyfin slideshow → tap/click to dismiss, no password
    
    Sources:
    - [Kscreenlocker no-password auth failure bug - KDE Discuss](https://discuss.kde.org/t/kscreenlocker-no-password-screen-unlock-will-generate-authentication-failure/19257)
  </RESPONSE>

